318: - `posse` validates the effective config on every call and refuses with `config_invalid` naming the file, key and reason.
319: - The built-in kind defaults above can be overridden but not removed; an unknown kind with no `[kinds.<kind>]` table runs with no extra args, `steer = false`, and no interrupt key sequence. `kinds.<kind>.disabled = true` prevents selecting that kind for new Worker dispatch only. It does not remove the kind's launch configuration, block an existing Lead, or affect historical Task readers and frozen relaunches.
320: - `defaults.max_workers` and `defaults.recovery_attempts` must stay positive. `pool.keep_idle = 0` is valid and retains no idle Sandboxes.
321: - The Lead workspace label is `Lead:<project>`. New Workers in every Project kind open as unfocused `<name>` tabs in the Lead workspace, with pane identity and tree `posse_row` metadata. Git worktree registration and locking remain unchanged. Existing linked-child Tasks retain their workspace label and linked-child Teardown behavior until completion; recovery and relaunch open them as Lead tabs. Posse never renames a User tab or workspace ([ADR 0013](adr/0013-all-workers-open-as-lead-tabs.md)).
322: - A Profile's `model` and `effort` reach the agent only through the kind's `model_args` and `effort_args` templates; a kind without a template for a set value is a `config_invalid` error naming the Profile.
323: - A Profile's `fallback` names another Profile. It must exist, and following fallbacks from any Profile must end without returning to it; otherwise `config_invalid` names the Profile. See Quota fallback in section 17.
324: - `review_provider` and `repositories.<member>.review_provider` are User-only settings that select the reviewer for newly admitted Tasks. The code default is `builtin`; a Member override takes precedence over the Project value. Admission freezes the selected provider in the Task plan, so later config changes do not move admitted Tasks to another reviewer. To roll back, run `posse config set review_provider builtin` globally or with `--project <name>`, and set any Member override to `builtin` or unset it.
325: - `defaults.review`, `repositories.<member>.review`, `[pool] setup`, `[autonomy]`, `notify.command`, kind argument templates (`auto_approve_args`, `model_args`, `effort_args`, `resume_args`, `system_prompt_args`, `lead_args`), kind trust preparation (`prepare`), `lead_auto_approve`, and Profile `kind`, `args` and `attribution` are User-only. `posse config set|unset` requires a registered Lead to pass `--user-approved "<User's words>"` with a non-blank quote. The Lead records the quote, key, action, set value and Project ID in `config_approvals` (global changes are attributed to its Project). A Worker cannot write any key, even with the flag. Pane bindings in the current process and its ancestors recognize registered native Worker and Lead children. An unbound pane or recognized agent environment/process is not the User and cannot write protected settings or answer Decisions. A plain User shell needs no quote; an unbound Herdr pane must first be observed without an agent. Missing or unavailable pane evidence does not prove it is a shell.
326: - These checks are cooperative same-user safeguards, not authentication. Environment/process hints and a consent quote can be forged by a process with the User's OS permissions; absence of a binding does not prove a human is present. The quote records provenance, not authenticated consent. Isolated test homes do not inherit parent actor hints across their home boundary.
327: - `posse config show --effective` includes effective values and per-key `sources`: built-in defaults, global config or Project config. Project scalar aliases resolve to canonical keys, concatenated Dispatch Rules list both contributing files, and derived yolo grants name the yolo source. `posse doctor` shows the selected implicit-dispatch policy and disabled kinds, plus separate active Worker, tracked native-child and parked-resource counts per Project. The native-child count is the Project's registered and running research attempts (section 26); it never includes unregistered harness subprocesses. Parked resources are held Sandboxes whose Tasks are outside active execution states.
328: - Config is written through `posse config set|unset` and `posse lowkey on|off`, which validate against the schema, write atomically and keep comments. `posse config schema` prints every key with its type, allowed values, default, meaning and whether it is `user_only`.
329: - Claude, codex and OpenCode Leads get their kind's `auto_approve_args` by default, including after restart. Set `kinds.<kind>.lead_auto_approve = false` to keep that harness's approval prompts; Profile `args` may still add flags. This changes only harness permissions, not Posse autonomy: review and merging decisions remain governed by `[autonomy]`. Pi is unchanged because it has no permission system. A Lead also gets `lead_args`: codex's default `workspace-write` sandbox cannot write the posse home (SQLite in WAL mode needs write access even to read), the Sandboxes or the agents' trust files, so it also runs with `--sandbox danger-full-access`.
330: - Agent attribution is off by default. Claude Leads and Workers start with `--settings '{"attribution":{"commit":"","pr":""},"includeCoAuthoredBy":false}'` placed before Profile `args`, so a Profile's own `--settings` replaces it. Every push of a Task branch (`posse publish`, candidate promotion and legacy Merge pushes) first reads the trailers of commits above the Task base and `origin/<target>` and refuses with `agent_attribution_refused`, listing each commit, when a `Co-authored-by`, `Assisted-by` or `Generated-by` trailer names an agent (Claude, Codex, Copilot, Gemini and similar, or an Anthropic or OpenAI email domain). Posse never rewrites history; the Worker rewords its commits. A Profile with `attribution = true` (User-only) keeps Claude's attribution and skips the check for Tasks admitted on it.
